---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import EvidenceStrip from "../../components/EvidenceStrip.astro";
import MeetingFlow from "../../components/MeetingFlow.astro";
import { projects } from "../../data/projects";

const project = projects.find((p) => p.id === "notetaker")!;
---

<Base
  title="Notetaker, Jamal Mammadzada"
  description="A case study on a consent-first native macOS app that captures meeting audio, transcribes on-device, and writes structured Markdown notes."
  progress={true}
>
  <article>
    <header class="wrap case-head">
      <p class="mono"><a href="/work/">Work</a> / Case study {project.index}</p>
      <h1 class="h-xl case-title">Notetaker</h1>
      <p class="lede narrow case-lede">
        A native macOS menu-bar app that records the meeting you explicitly
        approve, transcribes it on-device, and leaves behind notes you can edit.
        Its most important feature is the recording it refuses to start by itself.
      </p>

      <dl class="meta-grid case-meta">
        {project.meta.map((m) => <div><dt>{m.label}</dt><dd>{m.value}</dd></div>)}
        <div><dt>Built</dt><dd>{project.years}</dd></div>
      </dl>

      <p class="note case-note">
        The repository is private. This case study documents the architecture,
        privacy boundary, and verified behavior without publishing meeting data,
        prompts, credentials, or private source.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="wrap case-proof">
      <EvidenceStrip
        label="Notetaker evidence"
        items={[
          { value: "~1,300", label: "Swift lines", detail: "Five source files plus three test harnesses" },
          { value: "0.78 → 0.0", label: "Amplitude bug", detail: "One flag silently killed system audio" },
          { value: "2 signals", label: "Meeting detection", detail: "Helper process plus window title" },
          { value: "1 text pass", label: "Leaves the Mac", detail: "Transcript only, after you stop" },
        ]}
      />
    </div>

    <section class="wrap section narrow" aria-labelledby="boundary-first">
      <h2 class="h-lg sec-title" id="boundary-first">The boundary comes first</h2>
      <p>
        Meeting automation has an unusually sharp failure mode: convenience can
        become silent recording. Notetaker treats detection and consent as two
        different events. Seeing Zoom's in-meeting helper process or a live
        meeting title is enough to show a prompt. It is never enough to begin capture.
      </p>
      <p>
        In the code there is exactly one path that begins capture, and it is
        reachable from two places: the menu bar item you click, and the button
        in a dialog you answered. Detection can raise that dialog. It cannot
        answer it. That asymmetry is on purpose: a recorder that starts
        itself in a 1:1 or an interview is not a bug you get to apologize for
        afterward.
      </p>

      <p>
        Stopping is the opposite. Leaving the meeting ends the recording on its
        own, since forgetting to press stop costs you nothing worse than a longer
        file, while forgetting to press start costs you the meeting. Convenience
        belongs on whichever side of a decision is harmless when it fires by
        accident.
      </p>

      <p>
        That distinction survives the whole design. Audio stays on the Mac.
        whisper.cpp produces a local transcript. Only after recording stops does
        a single text-only request turn that transcript into decisions and action
        items. The editable Markdown result stays beside the source transcript.
      </p>

      <MeetingFlow />
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="system-shape">
      <h2 class="h-lg sec-title narrow" id="system-shape">A small app with a real pipeline</h2>
      <div class="architecture">
        <div class="arch-card reveal">
          <p class="mono">Capture</p>
          <h3 class="h-sm">ScreenCaptureKit</h3>
          <p>
            The stream combines system audio, the people on the other side, with
            microphone input. A discarded 2×2 pixel region satisfies the capture
            API without turning the app into a screen recorder.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="arch-card reveal">
          <p class="mono">Normalize</p>
          <h3 class="h-sm">WAVWriter</h3>
          <p>
            Audio is converted to 16 kHz mono WAV so the transcription stage gets
            one predictable format regardless of the devices active in the meeting.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="arch-card reveal">
          <p class="mono">Understand</p>
          <h3 class="h-sm">whisper.cpp → Markdown</h3>
          <p>
            Transcription runs locally. A meeting topic, sanitized before it can
            become a folder name, gives the final summarizer enough context to
            produce a useful heading and clearer notes.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section narrow" aria-labelledby="verified">
      <h2 class="h-lg sec-title" id="verified">The bug that looked like success</h2>
      <p>
        Audio software can hand back a perfectly valid file while recording the
        wrong thing. ScreenCaptureKit has a flag called
        <code>excludesCurrentProcessAudio</code>, and setting it true reads like
        obvious hygiene: don't capture your own app's output. On macOS 26 it
        silently kills the entire system-audio tap.
      </p>

      <p>
        The measured peak amplitude of the system channel drops from 0.78 to
        exactly 0.0. Every surface-level check still passes. The file exists, the
        RIFF header is well formed, the duration is right, and the waveform looks
        normal because your microphone is still feeding it. What you have lost is
        every other participant. With headphones on, the failure is total and
        invisible: you record yourself saying "mm-hm" for forty
        minutes and nothing else.
      </p>

      <p>
        That is the reason the WAV harness drives the writer with two synthetic
        tones instead of asserting that a file was produced. It starts a 440 Hz
        tone at zero seconds and an 880 Hz tone one second later, then measures
        RMS in three windows: the first source alone, the overlap, and the
        second source alone. The overlap has to be meaningfully louder than either tone
        by itself, which is only true if mixing actually happened. It also checks
        that summed peaks clamp instead of wrapping around, because a sign
        inversion at the top of the range is the kind of thing you hear as a
        click and never find by reading code.
      </p>

      <div class="checks">
        <div><span class="mono">01 · Capture</span><p>Inspect the output structure and measured signal, not just file creation.</p></div>
        <div><span class="mono">02 · Detection</span><p>Exercise the meeting signal separately from the permission to record.</p></div>
        <div><span class="mono">03 · Titles</span><p>Test slashes, colons, and newlines before calendar text becomes a path.</p></div>
      </div>

      <details class="tech">
        <summary>What the app won't guess</summary>
        <p>
          whisper.cpp does not provide reliable speaker diarization here, so the
          note prompt only attributes a statement when context makes the speaker
          clear. A missing label beats a confident wrong one.
        </p>
      </details>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section narrow" aria-labelledby="deadends">
      <h2 class="h-lg sec-title" id="deadends">Two approaches that don't work</h2>

      <p>
        Both of these look correct in documentation and fail against a real
        machine. They cost me more time than the parts that worked, so they are
        written down.
      </p>

      <details class="tech">
        <summary>Asking the audio system whether the microphone is in use</summary>
        <p>
          <code>kAudioDevicePropertyDeviceIsRunningSomewhere</code> sounds like
          exactly the right meeting signal. If something is holding the
          microphone, you are probably in a call, and it would work across every
          platform at once without caring which app is running.
        </p>
        <p>
          On macOS 26 it reports false while an application is actively
          recording. I checked it against ffmpeg holding the microphone open, so
          this is measured, not inferred. Any detector built on
          it would have failed closed and never prompted at all.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="tech">
        <summary>Reading Zoom's own meeting data</summary>
        <p>
          Zoom keeps meeting databases under its application support directory,
          which would supply participant lists and real scheduled topics instead
          of whatever the window happens to be called. Every one of those files
          is encrypted on disk.
        </p>
        <p>
          That leaves the window title as the only local source of a meeting
          name. Getting participants would mean a Zoom OAuth application and
          cloud API calls, which trades a local-only tool for one that needs
          account credentials and network access. For an app whose entire premise
          is that your audio never leaves the machine, that is a bad trade, so
          the feature stays missing.
        </p>
      </details>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section narrow" aria-labelledby="limits">
      <h2 class="h-lg sec-title" id="limits">What remains unsolved</h2>
      <p>
        This is a useful personal tool, not a general meeting platform. Rebuilding
        the app can cause macOS to ask for capture permission again because the
        code identity changes. Transcription speed and quality depend on the local
        model and the audio. Participant names are unavailable without adding a
        Zoom cloud integration, which would create a much larger privacy and
        authentication surface than this app currently needs.
      </p>
    </section>

    <nav class="wrap section case-next" aria-label="More work">
      <a href="/work/frcmob/" class="next-link">
        <span class="mono">Back to the first case study</span>
        <span class="h-md">FRCMOB →</span>
      </a>
    </nav>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .case-head { padding-top: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4.5rem); }
  .case-head > .mono { margin-bottom: 1.4rem; }
  .case-title { margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  .case-lede { margin-bottom: 2.5rem; }
  .case-meta { max-width: 38rem; margin-bottom: 1.75rem; }
  .case-note { max-width: 62ch; }
  .case-proof { margin-top: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4.5rem); }
  .sec-title { margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }

  .architecture {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 1px;
    background: var(--rule);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .arch-card { background: var(--paper-2); padding: clamp(1.1rem, 2.5vw, 1.6rem); }
  .arch-card .mono { color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .arch-card h3 { margin-bottom: 0.8rem; }
  .arch-card p:last-child { color: var(--ink-2); font-size: var(--fs-ui); }

  .checks { display: grid; gap: 0.7rem; margin: 1.7rem 0; }
  .checks > div { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 8rem 1fr; gap: 1rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-top: 0.8rem; }
  .checks .mono { color: var(--accent); font-size: var(--fs-label); }
  .checks p { margin: 0; color: var(--ink-2); font-size: var(--fs-ui); }

  code {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.86em;
    background: var(--accent-wash);
    color: var(--accent-ink);
    padding: 0.12em 0.36em;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    /* Long API identifiers must not push the column wider on small screens. */
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .case-next { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .next-link { display: grid; gap: 0.5rem; text-decoration: none; color: inherit; padding: 1.5rem 0; }
  .next-link .h-md { transition: color 0.16s ease; }
  .next-link:hover .h-md { color: var(--accent); }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .architecture { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .checks > div { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0.35rem; }
  }
</style>
